        },
        {
          "type": "notebook_cells",
          "attributes": {
            "definition": {
              "type": "markdown",
              "text": "## 7. Error budget consequences\n\nAttach the SLO status for the incident window here, from the SLO page for each objective. Three objectives exist:\n\n- **Voyager — Booking success rate** — metric-based, 99.0% over 30 days. This is the one the burn-rate alerts watch.\n- **Voyager — Search p95 under 800 ms** — monitor-based, 99.0% over 7 days.\n- **Voyager — Checkout availability** — time-slice from the checkout browser synthetic, 99.0% over 30 days.\n\nRecord the budget **consumed by this incident**, not the remaining budget: the remaining figure changes every day and will be meaningless to anyone reading this in a month. If the incident consumed a large share, that is itself an action item — either the system needs work or the objective was never achievable.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Root cause\n\nThe mechanism, not the trigger. \"A chaos flag was set\" and \"the deploy went out\" are triggers; the root cause is why the system could not absorb them. Keep asking why until the answer is something that can be changed.\n\n## 9. What went well\n\nGenuinely — which signal caught it, which dashboard answered the question first, what was already in place that saved time. This section is how good monitoring gets kept rather than quietly deleted in the next cleanup.\n\n## 10. What did not\n\nWhere time was lost. Missing instrumentation, a monitor that did not exist, an alert that went to nobody, a dashboard that showed no data because a metric name was wrong. Be specific about the minutes lost; it is the only way action items get prioritised honestly.\n\n## 11. Action items\n\nEvery row needs a named owner and a date. A postmortem with unowned actions is a diary entry.\n\n| Action | Type | Owner | Due | Ticket |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | Prevent / Detect / Mitigate / Process | | | |\n\n**Prevent** stops it happening. **Detect** shortens the detection gap from the timeline. **Mitigate** shortens the recovery. **Process** fixes how people responded. If every action item is \"Prevent\", the detection gap will be exactly the same next time something new breaks — and something new always does.\n\n## 12. Lessons for the demo\n\nVoyager-specific, and worth keeping. If this incident produced a better narrative than one of the ten scenarios in 01-PRD.md § 8, write down the flag combination that would reproduce it. If a dashboard widget was missing or misleading, say which one, because the fix belongs in `datadog/dashboards/` and will be lost otherwise."
            }
          }
        }
